# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  .gitattributes — Bantu (.b) language recognition
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  The Bantu programming language uses the .b extension. GitHub's
#  Linguist doesn't yet recognize Bantu as a separate language — a
#  PR is pending with github-linguist/linguist to add it.
#
#  Until that PR merges, *.b files fall back to Linguist's existing
#  classification as "B" (the 1969 language by Ken Thompson).
#  Once Linguist accepts Bantu, this file can switch to:
#
#      *.b linguist-language=Bantu
#
#  and the language bar will automatically show "Bantu" instead of "B".
#
#  Reference:
#    https://github.com/github-linguist/linguist
#    https://github.com/github-linguist/linguist/blob/master/docs/overrides.md

# 1) Bantu source files: UTF-8, LF endings, diffable as text
*.b   text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
*.b   diff

# 2) The bantu-src/ tree is the vendored interpreter source (kept in
#    the repo so the Dockerfile can build Bantu from scratch). Mark
#    it as vendored so Linguist doesn't skew the language stats —
#    only OUR application code (server.b, public/*.b) counts.
bantu-src/** linguist-vendored

# 3) Shell scripts
*.sh  text eol=lf

# 4) Windows batch files
*.bat text eol=crlf

# 5) Binary assets — never diff, never merge
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so  binary
*.dylib binary

# 6) Markdown docs
*.md  text eol=lf

# 7) YAML / JSON configs
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
